stdoutTail:
I fixed all four complaints so the code matches `docs/invitation-rules.md`. All 17 tests pass: the 10 existing ones and 7 new ones.

`npm test` can't run in this sandbox because the test runner can't spawn subprocesses. I ran the tests in-process with `node --test --test-isolation=none` instead.

- **Wrong role:** the role is now fixed when the invite is sent, using the inviter's pick or the org default at that moment. Before, an unspecified role was looked up at accept time, so later default changes leaked in. The change is in `roles.js` and `service.js`.
- **Invites dying on time:** an invite is now valid through its expiry instant and only expired when `now` is strictly greater. The check in `expiry.js` was `>=`.
- **Re-invite reviving dead invites:** the store used to keep one record per invitee, so a new invite replaced the old record and its status, which is how a revoked link came back. It now keeps every invite by token and tracks which one is current. Old tokens report `superseded`, and a revoked one keeps reporting `revoked` (change in `store.js`). Acceptance now checks statuses in the documented order, including `superseded`, which was missing.
- **Spelling of the address:** `emailsMatch` was a plain `===`. Matching is now done on canonical addresses, so `Sam+Eng@Acme.COM` and `sam@acme.com` are the same person. Invite, token, revoke and accept all use the canonical form, and an address with no `@` no longer gets mangled.

The new tests in `test/rules.test.js` check:
- the token examples from the docs (`inv_d467975a`, `inv_d36795c7`);
- the expiry boundary;
- plus-tag spellings;
- email mismatch;
- the status ordering;
- revoke behaviour;
- role snapshots on re-invite.
